# The law rank/composition_closure.sqlc owes: the null space of a whole descent is the sum of the
# null spaces of the levels in it. One row.
#
# The identity
#   Composing onto maps composes their null spaces additively, so under any root the dimension of
#   the composite map's null space is the sum of the blocks below it. The composite is onto a
#   single figure, so its own dimension is the leaf count less one, and the two routes are
#   `leaves - 1` and `Σ (parts - 1)` over the distinct fusions in the closure.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `kernel_closes`.
#
# What it can catch, which is not the arithmetic
#   On a tree the identity is forced by counting edges, so the content of this law is that the
#   descent under each root IS a tree. A leaf reached by a second path raises the block sum and
#   leaves the leaf count alone; a cycle takes the leaf count away without taking the blocks. Both
#   land here, and this law names the root, which is what neither of the instruments that already
#   exist does: checks/jagged_layer.sqlc names the fusion whose own parts overlap and
#   algebra/cycle_space.sqlc reports one number for the corpus.
#
# And the row space of the closure, in the same row
#   The composite is onto one figure, so `row_dim` is 1 at any depth, and every leaf reaches the
#   root through a path product that is strictly positive or that nobody stated. Those two
#   possibilities must exhaust the leaves: a path product of zero would be a factor of zero, which
#   `pm.part`'s own CHECK refuses, and a negative one is not a conversion. So this clause is the
#   block-level positivity carried through the nesting, and it is what says no non-negative
#   assignment to the leaves is invisible to the root.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `fold_subspaces`.
#
# Non-vacuity needs the closure to be a closure
#   At one level `leaves - 1` and `Σ (parts - 1)` are the same expression, so a corpus of
#   single-level compositions passes this law having compared nothing with itself. The floor
#   requires a root whose descent reaches two levels, which is where the sum over levels first has
#   more than one term.
#
# Every fusion, not only the tops
#   rank/composition_closure.sqlc takes each fusion as a root of its own closure, so a defect
#   under an interior fusion is reported at that fusion and at every fusion above it. Both are
#   true: the identity fails for the interior root on its own, and for each ancestor because its
#   closure contains the interior one.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `composition_closure`. In a rolled-back transaction, give a fusion a
#   part naming a layer already reachable beneath it through another composition, and this law must
#   fail at that fusion and at nothing that does not contain it.
-- rank/composition_closure.sqlc: the composite's dimension against the sum of its levels'.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/composition_closure' AS subject,
           count(*) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.deepest >= 2) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.closure_kernel <> c.sum_kernel) = 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.row_dim <> 1) = 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.leaves_positive + c.leaves_unknown <> c.leaves) = 0
                                                                                     AS holds,
           format('%s root(s), %s reaching two levels or more; %s disagree between the '
                  'composite dimension and the sum of its blocks, %s have a leaf whose path '
                  'product is neither positive nor unstated, %s leaf arrival(s) unstated%s',
                  count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.deepest >= 2),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.closure_kernel <> c.sum_kernel),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.leaves_positive + c.leaves_unknown <> c.leaves),
                  coalesce(sum(c.leaves_unknown), 0),
                  coalesce(': ' || string_agg(format('%s/%s wants %s and sums to %s',
                                                     c.composition, c.composed_layer,
                                                     c.closure_kernel, c.sum_kernel),
                                              ', ' ORDER BY c.composition, c.composed_layer)
                                   FILTER (WHERE c.closure_kernel <> c.sum_kernel), ''))
                                                                                     AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(rank/composition_closure.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'composition_closure'
